description:
CSS scroll-driven animations represent a major advancement in web animation technology, allowing CSS keyframe animations to be directly linked to scroll position or element visibility instead of time. This cutting-edge feature eliminates the need for JavaScript scroll event listeners and Intersection Observers, running animations on the compositor thread for buttery-smooth 60fps performance.

## What It Is

Scroll-driven animations use the new `animation-timeline` CSS property to connect animations to scroll-based timelines. Instead of animations progressing over time (as with `animation-duration`), they progress based on how far the user has scrolled through content. This creates seamless, interactive scroll effects that feel natural and responsive.

The feature includes three main timeline types:

### 1. Scroll Progress Timelines (`scroll()` function)
- Driven by the scroll position of a scrollable container
- 0% = top/start of scroll, 100% = bottom/end
- Can animate based on vertical (block), horizontal (inline), or specific axes
- Perfect for reading progress bars, parallax effects, and continuous scroll animations

### 2. View Progress Timelines (`view()` function)
- Driven by when elements enter or exit the viewport
- 0% = element enters viewport (bottom edge visible)
- 50% = element centered in viewport
- 100% = element exits viewport (top edge leaves)
- Ideal for reveal-on-scroll effects, fade-ins, and viewport-triggered animations

### 3. Named Timelines
- Use `scroll-timeline-name` and `view-timeline-name` to create reusable timelines
- Multiple elements can share the same timeline for coordinated effects
- Enables complex scroll-linked patterns like section navigation highlighting

## Key Properties

### animation-timeline
```css
.element {
  animation: fadeIn linear;
  animation-timeline: scroll();           /* Nearest scrollable ancestor */
  animation-timeline: scroll(root);      /* Document root */
  animation-timeline: scroll(nearest);   /* Nearest ancestor (default) */
  animation-timeline: scroll(self);      /* Element itself */
  animation-timeline: view();            /* When element enters/exits view */
  animation-timeline: --my-timeline;     /* Named timeline */
}
```

### animation-range
Control which portion of the timeline triggers the animation:
```css
.element {
  animation-timeline: scroll();
  animation-range: 0% 50%;              /* First half of scroll */
  animation-range: entry 0% entry 100%; /* While entering viewport */
  animation-range: cover 0% cover 100%; /* Entire time in viewport */
}
```

## Why It Matters

### Performance Benefits
- **Compositor-thread animations**: No main thread blocking, guaranteed 60fps
- **No JavaScript overhead**: Eliminates scroll event listeners and IntersectionObserver
- **No layout thrashing**: Only animates transform and opacity (GPU-accelerated)
- **Deterministic timing**: Animation progress is tied to scroll position, not time

### Development Benefits
- **Declarative**: Pure CSS solution, easier to understand and maintain
- **Less code**: Often requires 50-70% less code than JavaScript equivalents
- **Predictable**: Behavior is tied to scroll position, not time-based timing functions
- **Progressive enhancement**: Easy to provide fallbacks for older browsers

### UX Benefits
- **Natural feel**: Animations respond directly to user interaction
- **Reveal effects**: Perfect for storytelling through scroll
- **Progress indicators**: Reading progress bars, completion states
- **Spatial awareness**: Elements animate based on their position in the viewport

## Use Cases for OpenDrones

### Product Page Animations
- **Image reveal effects**: Drone images reveal smoothly as user scrolls through features
- **Parallax backgrounds**: Hero section with depth effects
- **Feature cards**: Fade-in, slide-up, or scale-up as they enter viewport
- **Technical specs**: Animated charts or data visualizations that reveal on scroll

### Landing Page Effects
- **Reading progress bar**: Visual indicator of content completion
- **Section navigation**: Nav items highlight when their section is in view
- **Scroll-triggered reveals**: Product benefits and use cases animate as user scrolls
- **Hero animations**: Drone models rotate or transform as user scrolls down

### Interactive Components
- **Horizontal scroll galleries**: Product comparison or drone specifications
- **Counter animations**: Numbers count up when sections become visible
- **Image sequences**: Step-by-step drone operation animations
- **Progress indicators**: Checkout flow or tutorial progress bars

## Browser Support (2025)

### Supported (Chromium-based)
- **Chrome 115+** (July 2023)
- **Edge 115+** (July 2023)
- **Opera 101+** (August 2023)

### In Development
- **Safari 18+**: Native support coming (late 2024)
- **Firefox**: Feature flag available, implementation in progress

### Production Considerations
- Always use feature detection: `@supports (animation-timeline: scroll())`
- Provide progressive enhancement with JavaScript fallbacks
- Consider using the official scroll-timeline polyfill for unsupported browsers
- Test thoroughly on Safari (especially iOS Safari)

## Fallback Strategies

### Progressive Enhancement Pattern
```css
/* Base state - works everywhere */
.element {
  opacity: 0;
  transform: translateY(20px);
  transition: all 0.6s ease-out;
}

.element.is-visible {
  opacity: 1;
  transform: translateY(0);
}

/* Modern browsers with scroll-timeline */
@supports (animation-timeline: view()) {
  .element {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: none;
    transition: none;
    animation: fadeInUp linear;
    animation-timeline: view();
    animation-range: entry 0% entry 100%;
  }

  .element.is-visible {
    /* Override JS class in modern browsers */
    opacity: 1;
    transform: none;
    transition: none;
  }
}
```

### JavaScript Fallback with IntersectionObserver
```javascript
// Only load if scroll-timeline not supported
if (!CSS.supports('animation-timeline', 'scroll()')) {
  const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
    entries.forEach(entry => {
      if (entry.isIntersecting) {
        entry.target.classList.add('is-visible');
      }
    });
  }, { threshold: 0.1 });

  document.querySelectorAll('.animate-on-scroll').forEach(el => {
    observer.observe(el);
  });
}
```

### Official Polyfill
```html
<script src="https://flackr.github.io/scroll-timeline/dist/scroll-timeline.js"></script>
```

## Accessibility

Always respect user preferences:
```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .scroll-animated {
    animation: none;
    opacity: 1;
    transform: none;
  }
}
```

## Best Practices

### ✅ DO
- Use `animation-fill-mode: forwards` for reveal effects
- Animate only transform and opacity (GPU-accelerated)
- Set `animation-duration: 1ms` when using scroll-driven animations
- Provide meaningful fallbacks for older browsers
- Test performance on mobile devices
- Keep animations subtle and purposeful

### ❌ DON'T
- Animate layout properties (width, height, margin, padding)
- Use complex animations on scroll
- Forget about accessibility
- Assume universal browser support
- Create jarring or distracting effects

## Performance Tips

```css
/* GPU-accelerated properties only */
@keyframes performant {
  from {
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(20px) scale(0.95);
  }
  to {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: translateY(0) scale(1);
  }
}

/* Use will-change sparingly */
.element {
  will-change: transform, opacity;
}
```

## Examples

### Reading Progress Bar
```css
.reading-progress {
  position: fixed;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  height: 4px;
  background: linear-gradient(to right, #667eea, #764ba2);
  transform-origin: left;
  animation: grow-progress linear;
  animation-timeline: scroll(root block);
}

@keyframes grow-progress {
  from { transform: scaleX(0); }
  to { transform: scaleX(1); }
}
```

### Fade-In on Viewport Entry
```css
.card {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: card-fade-in ease-out forwards;
  animation-timeline: view();
  animation-range: entry 0% entry 50%;
}

@keyframes card-fade-in {
  from {
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(30px) scale(0.95);
  }
  to {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: translateY(0) scale(1);
  }
}
```

### Section Navigation Highlight
```css
section {
  view-timeline-name: var(--section-name);
  view-timeline-axis: block;
}

.nav-link[href="#features"] {
  animation: navHighlight linear;
  animation-timeline: --features;
  animation-range: entry 0% exit 100%;
}

@keyframes navHighlight {
  0%, 100% { opacity: 0.5; border-color: transparent; }
  10%, 90% { opacity: 1; border-color: #667eea; }
}
```

## Framework Integration

### React
```jsx
// ScrollTimelineAnimation.jsx
import { useEffect, useRef } from 'react';

export default function ScrollTimelineAnimation({ children }) {
  const ref = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (CSS.supports('animation-timeline', 'view()')) {
      ref.current?.classList.add('scroll-timeline-active');
    }
  }, []);

  return (
    <div ref={ref} className="scroll-animated">
      {children}
    </div>
  );
}
```

